WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.830
Speaker 1: Today we'll be speaking with Saul
Cassin, who's distinguished professor of psychology

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.960
at John Jay College of Criminal Justice in
New York City, and also professor emeritus

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.330
of psychology at Williams College in Massachusetts.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:18.930
He is author of the book Duped Why Innocent
People Confess and Why We Believe Their Confessions.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.010
I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.200
Glad to have you on.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.610
Speaker 6: I'm very, very happy to be here.

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:32.300
Speaker 1: So anecdotally, we all hear stories
of innocent people who confessed to crimes

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.720
they did not commit.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.340
I think a natural reaction, a common reaction
is to say, I would never do that.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.720
I can't imagine any circumstances in which
police sit me down and they suspect me for

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.720
a crime.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.790
And I know I didn't commit it.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.910
I would never, ever, ever confess to a crime
he didn't commit.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.270
And yet it happens all the time.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.280
Can you tell us a little bit about why this
happens?

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.210
Speaker 6: You're absolutely right.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.140
I think the first myth that has to be debunked
is I would never confess to a crime I did

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.140
not commit.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.140
Yeah.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.630
Answer Yes, you would.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.880
And you will note that there are two titles
to the title of my book.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.240
One is Why Innocent People Confess, and two
Why We Believe Their Confessions.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.700
We are duped into believing false confessions.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.600
So when those of us who are presented with
a confession and we're thinking an innocent

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.070
person would never do this.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.230
We can't see past a false confession.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.230
It looks true.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.230
It sounds true.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.230
It feels true.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.520
I would never do that.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.299
Innocent people don't do that wrong.

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:40.189
Imagine yourself being brought in as a suspect
in a crime.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:42.180
You're taken into the station, you're alone.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.180
You're isolated.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.180
You're not with family.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.180
You're not with friends.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.860
If you're a kid, you're asking, can I call
my mother?

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.710
And the answer is not to We're done here.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:56.280
And then you come in and the detective walks
in and slaps a folder on the table and says,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.189
We know you committed this crime and we have
a boatload of evidence against you.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:06.610
And that's how that session proceeds privately
without you having any access to outside sources

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.920
of information or people.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.600
And that goes on sometimes for hours.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.629
Until the officer, the detective or detectives,
plural, offers you something of a way out.

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:19.160
You know, I think you're a good person.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.620
I don't think you intended to do this.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.400
Maybe this was just something your friends
put you up to her, or you had one too many

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.760
drinks, and they start to make it sound as
if the consequence would be minimal.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.410
You want out, you believe.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.470
For whatever reason, they seem to have this
overwhelming evidence against you.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.930
And you've been denying this for hours, and
they keep calling you a liar.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:47.020
Every time you deny it, you're trying to get
out of the situation.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.920
And now they're offering you an escape hatch,
the escape hatches.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.489
You know what?

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:52.659
Just cooperate with us.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:54.180
We think we think you're a good person.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.840
We don't think you meant to do this.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.170
And before you know what, you're signing a
statement believing that that signature and

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.870
that statement is in your best interest.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.440
Speaker 1: It sounds like to some degree.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:12.569
You know, we've talked with attorneys about
the kind of plea deal industrial complex and

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:19.440
some of the circumstances, including being
able to get out of pretrial detention, including

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:25.599
a known smaller penalty than chancing the
possibility of a much stricter penalty.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:26.680
We've talked about that.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:33.420
It seems that there are some similar dynamics
between that and false confessions, although

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.900
they clearly are two different things and
they relate to the isolation, the disproportionate

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.720
power, that dynamic that exists and the unknown
of it could be even worse if I stick to my

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.720
guns.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:47.989
Speaker 6: That's exactly right.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:53.379
And what we're a perfect parallel to plea
bargaining that you just described, because

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:57.250
they are the same process people are making.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.689
What a decision making theorist would say
is a rational decision, given the information

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:02.689
they have.

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:05.099
They're trying at this point to cut their
losses.

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:09.560
And you know what's interesting about the
parallel between the confession that a suspect

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.540
gives to police in an interrogation room and
then the guilty plea confession that they

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.450
give in court to prosecutors and defense lawyers.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.840
It's the same dynamic.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.020
But here's what's interesting.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.460
People who have confessed to police are then
later more likely to accept the false guilty

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:29.460
plea.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.460
Why?

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:36.120
Because everybody correctly is telling them
if you go to trial, you're going to get convicted.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.680
So the confession now gives rise to and spawns
a false guilty plea.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.240
And, you know, I've got a whole chapter in
this book about this system of ours being

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.430
obsessed with confession.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.840
It happens one more time.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.919
Imagine the person who confesses, later pleads
guilty, is sitting in prison and is now eligible

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:00.419
for parole, and they have to appear before
a parole board.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:01.660
Well, guess what?

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.950
They can't get out until they accept responsibility
and show they've been rehabilitated.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:12.220
And so I've spoken and worked on cases of
exonerees who confessed and then said the

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:19.699
most galling next thing I ever had to do in
my life was re confessed years later in order

00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:21.949
to become.

00:05:21.949 --> 00:05:23.069
Paroled.

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:27.720
And once again, here's the system not understanding
that the baseline level of wrongful conviction

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.460
it's not zero guys may have been conceived
of a zero 50 years ago.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:33.910
We now know it's not zero.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:39.410
Speaker 1: When there is a confession and
the confession isn't thrown out.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.479
When it's thrown out, we can put it in another
category.

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:49.490
But when there's a confession that makes it
into an eventual trial, is it overwhelmingly

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:55.319
difficult to to overcome that when there is
an accepted confession as part of a trial?

00:05:55.319 --> 00:06:00.699
Speaker 6: I once first of all, I seldom see
confessions that are suppressed from evidence.

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:04.120
Yeah, they almost always are admitted these
days.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.730
The question the judge will ask at a suppression
hearing is, well, did he waive his Miranda

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:09.730
rights?

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:10.730
That he did.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:11.730
He received waive his Miranda rights?

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:12.730
Yes.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.199
Well, then it's a voluntary statement.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:17.509
That's that becomes proof of voluntariness.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.770
Miranda is a whole separate problem.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:20.770
Yes.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.039
In this business.

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:26.690
So so now that now the person comes to court
and there's a confession in evidence.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:30.900
I don't want to overstate it, but almost nothing
else matters.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.570
I'm in the infamous Central Park jogger case,
for example.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:36.520
People don't realize this.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.349
Those kids confessed on videotape.

00:06:39.349 --> 00:06:43.650
They were induced after 14 to 30 hours of
questioning to confess.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.910
And only the confession was on tape, not the.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:49.990
Speaker 1: And just so people know, sometimes
this is known as the Central Park Five case.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:50.990
It's the state.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:51.990
And we're talking about the same case.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:52.990
Yeah.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:53.990
Speaker 6: Yes.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.840
And they've all been exonerated since exonerated.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:03.379
What people don't realize is after they confessed
and before they went to trial, the jogger

00:07:03.379 --> 00:07:07.270
was raped and there was a rape kit and there
were several semen samples.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.800
And those samples were sent to the crime lab.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:15.750
And when those samples came back, here was
the result, all of the different semen samples

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:20.449
traced to a single individual, not five, not
six, one person.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:24.720
And that person was not one of the five confessors.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.879
The confessors were excluded.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:30.720
So they come to trial with their confessions.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.190
No other supporting corroborating evidence,
contradictory DNA evidence, and they were

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.190
convicted.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.250
That's how powerful confession evidence is.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:43.190
Almost nothing else matters once that is in
evidence.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:51.590
Speaker 1: As a general question, how relevant
in these situations is some level of education

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:56.009
as well as the financial access to a lawyer?

00:07:56.009 --> 00:08:01.710
And the reason I asked that is, in my mind,
I would never even go in to talk to police

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:02.710
voluntarily.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:08.389
Number one, if I had to go in, I would absolutely
get a lawyer.

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:12.280
And if I was arrested and then, you know,
if it wasn't a situation where they call and

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:15.800
say, hey, would you like to come in if I'm
arrested and brought in?

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:21.270
I would say literally not a word other than
I want a lawyer, which seems like it would

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.500
help me in this situation or even when doing
that.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.460
Do you still see false confessions?

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:32.190
Speaker 6: Man you're asking all the press,
all the right questions.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:35.930
So, myth number one, I would never confess
to a crime I didn't commit.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.270
Myth number two is I'd know a false confession
if I saw one.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:43.050
If I sat on a jury and this was an innocent
person, you know, it would not.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.050
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:50.200
Speaker 6: The third myth is that Miranda
protects us that our Miranda rights matter.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:56.100
Here, here's here's one fact that was discovered
within one year of the Supreme Court's Miranda

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.740
decision that roughly 80% of people waive
their rights right out of the gate.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:01.740
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.500
Speaker 6: And we now know that that number
is holding steady and higher.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:07.500
Here's what else we know.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.920
You know who's the most likely to waive their
rights?

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:12.920
Innocent people.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:13.920
Speaker 2: Hmm.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.010
Speaker 6: When I work on cases involving
false confessors who were later fully DNA

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.170
exonerated without dispute, they're factually
innocent.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.000
I asked them two questions.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.560
One, why did you confess?

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.030
And the second question is when things got
ugly.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:29.920
Why don't you call a lawyer?

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.200
Why didn't you stop talking and call a lawyer?

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.240
Every one of them says the same thing in almost
the same words.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:39.240
I didn't need a lawyer.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.240
I didn't do anything wrong.

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.240
Hmm.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.050
Innocent people don't understand the peril.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:49.500
They have some fundamental belief in the power
of their innocence to prevail.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:54.870
They have some fundamental belief in the world
as a place that is more just than this.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:59.010
And so they figure, you know, I'll talk, we'll
talk, they'll see.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:06.100
I didn't do this very often when the police
imply or outright state, we have evidence.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.110
We've got prints we're sending off to the
lab.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.080
Now, that sounds like a threat.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.100
We got you.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.630
We've got prints to the innocent person.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.920
Imagine yourself in and you know you're innocent.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.709
They say they've got prints, you've been here
for hours and you want to get out and you're

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:24.120
thinking prints.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.500
That's going to show my innocence to the innocent
person.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.149
That threat, that bluff of having evidence.

00:10:31.149 --> 00:10:33.040
Is a promise of future exoneration.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.140
Speaker 5: Mm hmm.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:37.830
Speaker 6: And so they think, okay, I've been
here for 19 hours.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.079
I can't take it anymore.

00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:40.760
Where do I sign?

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.330
And when the prints come back, you'll see.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.330
I didn't do this.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.910
Speaker 1: I don't know the law around.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:48.190
You mentioned 19 hours, 13 hours, 14 hours.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.760
This is sort of an interlude, but I don't
know the law around that.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.120
But if you asked for a lawyer right away,
is a lawyer going to allow you to be there

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:00.019
for 19 hours or does a lawyer prevent that
from even becoming the situation?

00:11:00.019 --> 00:11:06.339
Speaker 6: A lawyer seldom people seldom ask
for a lawyer right away.

00:11:06.339 --> 00:11:10.860
And part of it, too, is and this gets to some
points about Miranda.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.400
First of all, people don't fully understand
their rights.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.250
And, you know, Miranda is something we hear.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:18.730
We hear it on Law and Order.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:23.120
Everybody can recite the words by heart, you
know, almost, almost by memory.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:28.360
And yet when you when you measure people's
comprehension of the rights themselves and

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.480
how they operate them, it's amazing how little
comprehension people have.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:39.870
So, for example, what if you come in and you
don't realize that you're a suspect and you

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:43.459
think maybe you're a cooperating witness and
you're not sure that the police are about

00:11:43.459 --> 00:11:45.389
to fully accuse you?

00:11:45.389 --> 00:11:49.280
Yeah, but they read you your Miranda rights
to say, we'd like to talk to you, but we can't

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.390
really do that until we get past this formality.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.420
And so I'd like to read your rights.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:58.160
You've heard them a million times before,
but you have the right to remain silent.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.959
Anything you say can be used against you.

00:12:00.959 --> 00:12:02.080
The suspect says yes.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.410
Yes, yes, I understand.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.750
Where do I sign?

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.430
And they sign away that waiver.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:11.830
Now, what if things start to get ugly?

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:17.391
Ask the average person, Are you able, once
you've signed a waiver of your rights, are

00:12:17.391 --> 00:12:20.880
you able to re invoke them later?

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.079
The answer is yes, right?

00:12:22.079 --> 00:12:23.769
A lot of people don't know.

00:12:23.769 --> 00:12:25.170
The answer is yes.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.170
Hmm.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:29.269
So there is one aspect of comprehension that
people lack.

00:12:29.269 --> 00:12:34.380
People think once they've signed off on that
waiver, that's it.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:37.730
And they worry also about the optics of silence.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:42.110
And, of course, for innocent people, they
don't understand the peril of talking.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:43.110
Right.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:44.110
And so they do.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:49.550
And so the people that require in some sense
the greatest protection, those accused who

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.940
are innocent, they're not going to use that
protection.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.730
And so it tends not to happen.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.209
Now, as far as the time frame you mentioned.

00:12:58.209 --> 00:13:02.100
The average interrogation only lasts for 30
minutes to an hour.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:07.080
And if you want to encompass 90 to 95 of all
percent of all interrogations, it's a couple

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.740
of hours.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:14.310
When you look at the database of wrongful
convictions in which people give false confessions,

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:19.970
now you're looking at 8 hours, 10 hours, 16
hours, 20 hours.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:22.199
Time breaks people down.

00:13:22.199 --> 00:13:25.389
Time breaks people down because we start to
get lonely.

00:13:25.389 --> 00:13:30.321
I don't mean that in a in a, in a clinical
sense, but people need others around and they

00:13:30.321 --> 00:13:33.540
need social support, especially when times
get stressful.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.620
Yeah, they need someone to check in with.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:37.260
They need breaks.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.260
They need to get.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:39.380
They need sleep.

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:43.550
Sleep deprivation is accompanied by these
20 hour sessions.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:46.180
Food breaks, you know.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.250
So it's both.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:51.980
It's both biological and social needs and
fatigue sets in.

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:58.420
I've worked on cases where I literally did
a count of the number of times the innocent

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.709
person said, I didn't do this and was called
the liar.

00:14:02.709 --> 00:14:03.709
Hmm.

00:14:03.709 --> 00:14:07.010
Sometimes that happens over 100 times in an
interrogation.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:12.080
So if you want out, if you're exhausted, if
you can't take it anymore, and every time

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:17.180
you deny involvement, they call you a liar
and you realize, this isn't my way out of

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:18.180
here.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.180
You've got to try something else.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.660
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask you about.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:25.000
You write in the book about the Amanda Knox
murder case in Italy.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.240
There's a Netflix documentary about that.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:33.449
And if I recall, generally speaking, this
is a case in which Amanda Knox, an American,

00:14:33.449 --> 00:14:38.210
was accused of a murder, along with I believe
it's a guy she may have been romantically

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:42.770
involved with of of a third person, a woman.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.880
Can you talk a little bit about what happened
in that one and how it's relevant to what

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:46.880
you write about?

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:53.089
Speaker 6: You know, it's such a perfect illustration
of something that's so important to know when

00:14:53.089 --> 00:14:55.529
people hear that an innocent person confessed.

00:14:55.529 --> 00:14:58.050
Knowing as you opened with, I would never
do that.

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.250
A normal person would never do that.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:03.259
Everyone's first reaction is, what's wrong
with that person?

00:15:03.259 --> 00:15:07.910
Yeah, that one is they immediately make an
attribution to something is wrong with that

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.540
person who did this.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:13.410
They don't think about the situation that
person was in that might afflict just about

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:14.649
anybody.

00:15:14.649 --> 00:15:18.470
Amanda Knox was a 20 year old American student
in Italy.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:21.000
Smart, smart kid.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.240
But she was in Italy.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:24.660
She wasn't fluent in the language.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:28.720
And her roommate was murdered in a vile way.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:33.250
And she was the person who discovered the
crime scene and called it in.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:39.940
And over the course of four days, for no good
reason, she was identified as a suspect for

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.329
no good reason.

00:15:41.329 --> 00:15:46.830
And I, I could catalog the the numbers of
demeanor cues.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.019
Oh, she did that.

00:15:48.019 --> 00:15:49.019
She looked like this.

00:15:49.019 --> 00:15:50.019
She looked like that.

00:15:50.019 --> 00:15:51.019
She didn't cry enough.

00:15:51.019 --> 00:15:52.350
She cried too much.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.490
That were used to identify her as a suspect.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:16:00.250
To the point where she was interrogated on
and off for four and a half days, for over

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:07.269
50 hours, culminating in a final night where
they brought her in, told her that that that

00:16:07.269 --> 00:16:13.310
romantic, the person she was romantically
involved with who was with her that night,

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:17.779
that he disavowed her alibi, that he said
he wasn't with her that night.

00:16:17.779 --> 00:16:20.480
Now it's now five in the morning.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:21.530
She's confused.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:24.210
She's been at this for days.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:29.150
Her mother, like her mother said, maybe we
should come and get you and bring you home,

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.980
which says, no, I think I can help them with
this.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:37.430
So she stayed back and had that phenomenology
of innocence where she didn't appreciate the

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:38.430
peril.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:39.430
She wasn't.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:40.430
She didn't.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:47.760
When I ask her about this, she didn't realize
she was a suspect until the very last moment.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:52.870
And then at five, six in the morning, they
handwrite a confession in Italian and get

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:54.100
her to sign it.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:56.410
And she confesses.

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:59.949
And then they take another confession a couple
hours later.

00:16:59.949 --> 00:17:05.799
And as soon as she as soon as she has a little
sense of mind and she's left alone, she immediately

00:17:05.799 --> 00:17:08.079
realizes, Oh, my God, I'm so confused.

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:11.530
And she hand writes of recantation of that
confession.

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:12.530
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:16.610
Speaker 6: But in the meantime, the Amanda
Knox case shows something else.

00:17:16.610 --> 00:17:21.970
She confesses within two weeks, there's DNA
splatter all over the crime scene.

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:25.579
They find the guy who did it and he has a
criminal background.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:27.390
And he fled to Germany right after the crime.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:32.850
I mean, they they got the guy, but they would
not overturn her confession.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:38.690
And they proceeded to prosecute her and the
boyfriend, despite having DNA all over the

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:41.270
place from another person.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:42.600
By the time she got to trial.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.450
And this this tells a lot about confession
cases.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:49.840
By the time she got to trial, it wasn't just
her confession.

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:51.539
Her friends turned against her.

00:17:51.539 --> 00:17:54.900
Some new witnesses came out of the woodwork.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:18:01.440
Forensic analysts were misinterpreting forensic
evidence in ways that suggested her guilt.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:06.330
It illustrates something other some other
point about confessions.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:09.090
When somebody confesses to a crime they didn't
commit.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:15.190
All of a sudden it corrupts that confession
once the word gets out, corrupts other witnesses,

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.929
corrupt forensic scientists.

00:18:16.929 --> 00:18:22.080
And so by the time a confessor and innocent
confessor gets into the courtroom, they face

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:27.220
a mountain of evidence, which is really nothing
but a house of cards built on the back of

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:28.800
a false confession.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.960
And that's what happened to her.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:35.990
She appeared in court, and all of a sudden
there's this parade of lying and non-credible

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:41.100
witnesses and forensic examiners were not
correctly interpreting the data.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:44.710
And, you know, her case has ultimately been
resolved.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:50.049
Italy's Supreme Court said so, declaring her
innocent was critical of the prosecutor for

00:18:50.049 --> 00:18:51.800
the case to begin with.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:54.610
Yeah, but but she went through hell.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:59.620
And and part of the reason she went through
hell was the constant surprise of witness

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:02.730
after witness who appeared in light of her
confession.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:03.730
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:08.640
Speaker 1: That's a quite a quite a emblematic
story of exactly what you write about in the

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:09.640
book.

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:14.159
I'll remind you, the book is Duped Why Innocent
People Confess and Why We Believe Their Confessions.

00:19:14.159 --> 00:19:17.000
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Saul Casson.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:18.500
Really appreciate your time today.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:19.570
Speaker 6: It's a pleasure.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:20.210
It's a pleasure.

